The panel discriminated significantly between the samples both in typicality
($n = <<n_complete_typ>>$ respondents with complete answers;
$Q = <<q_all>>$, $df = <<df_q_all>>$, $p <<p_q_all>>$) and in overall
acceptance ($n = <<n_complete_overall>>$; Friedman $\chi^2 = <<fr_all>>$,
$p <<p_fr_all>>$). After excluding P3, the differences among the remaining six
samples were not significant either for typicality ($Q = <<q_rest>>$,
$df = <<df_q_rest>>$, $p <<p_q_rest>>$) or for overall acceptance
($\chi^2 = <<fr_rest>>$, $p <<p_fr_rest>>$). The proportion of ``typical''
ratings differed significantly from 0.5 for only <<n_sig_binom>> of the
7~samples (P3) (Table~\ref{tab:panel_lab}).

\subsection{Organoleptic Assessment in the Laboratories}

The coded verdicts of the four laboratories are listed in
Table~\ref{tab:panel_lab}, and the full verbatim descriptions in
Table~\ref{tab:interlab_descriptions} (Appendix~\ref{app:interlab_raw}). Lab1,
Lab2 and Lab3 rated the odor or taste of samples <<lab_flagged>> as not typical
for honey, and samples <<lab_minor>> were flagged by one of them. No laboratory
raised objections to samples <<lab_none>>. For all samples, Lab4 issued the
verdict ``ambiguous characteristics'' (code L), and in its descriptions it
reported an odor characteristic of signs of fermentation in P1, P5 and P6, an
unnatural odor in P2 and a candy-like odor in P7. Full agreement of the four
laboratories on the binary scale was reached for <<n_full_agree_bin>> of the
7~samples ($\kappa = <<kappa_bin>>$, fair agreement), and on the three-level
scale for <<n_full_agree_ord>> of the 7~samples ($\kappa = <<kappa_ord>>$,
slight agreement). If the Lab4 descriptions indicating fermentation or an
unnatural or candy-like odor were coded as N, $\kappa$ on the binary scale would
be <<kappa_bin_alt>>, and the majority verdict of the laboratories
<<alt_majority_same>>. Coding only the taste descriptions yielded
<<taste_only_same>> coding the lower of the odor and taste scores.

\subsection{Comparison of the Panel with the Laboratory Results}

\textit{Panel as a rater.} Taken as a whole, the panel classified only sample
<<panel_T>> as typical and did not flag any sample
(Table~\ref{tab:panel_lab}). For the remaining six samples, the 95\% confidence
interval included 0.5 and the panel verdict remained inconclusive. The
laboratories flagged samples <<lab_flagged>> by majority (<<n_flags_major>> of
<<n_labs>> N ratings each). At the observed proportions of ``typical'' ratings,
the confidence interval would exclude 0.5 only with <<n_decide_P6>>
respondents for P6 and with <<n_decide_P7>> for P7.

